```python
def prefix_function(p):
    pi = [0] * len(p)
    k = 0
    for i in range(1, len(p)):
        while k > 0 and p[i] != p[k]:
            k = pi[k - 1]        # next shorter border of p[:i]
        if p[i] == p[k]:
            k += 1               # the border extends by one character
        pi[i] = k
    return pi
```

At position `i` we know the longest border of `p[:i]` has length `k`. It extends to a border of `p[:i+1]` exactly when the next character `p[i]` equals the character after the border, `p[k]`. If not, the next candidate is the longest border of that border, `pi[k - 1]`, because a border of a border is a border. The amortised argument from the lesson bounds the total work: `k` rises by at most one per step and every fall-back lowers it, so the whole loop is $O(m)$. That is why the 100,001-character test finishes instantly, while trying every border length would take about $m^2/2 = 5 \times 10^9$ steps.

